---
import IllustrationFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/IllustrationFigure.astro"
import ComparisonTable from "~/components/illustration-kit/ComparisonTable.astro"
import SideBySide from "~/components/illustration-kit/SideBySide.astro"
import Panel from "~/components/illustration-kit/Panel.astro"
---

<IllustrationFigure
  name="raven-paradox"
  lead="A green apple fits the second sentence, even though it tells us nothing obvious about ravens."
>
  <SideBySide>
    <Panel title="Rule about ravens" accent="blue">
      <p class="text-base">All ravens are black.</p>
    </Panel>
    <Panel title="Equivalent rule" accent="green">
      <p class="text-base">All non-black things are non-ravens.</p>
    </Panel>
  </SideBySide>
  <ComparisonTable
    caption="Classify an object by colour and whether it is a raven"
    columns={["", "Black", "Not black"]}
    rows={[
      ["Raven", "Black raven: fits the rule", "Non-black raven: would refute it"],
      ["Not a raven", "Black shoe", "<strong>Green apple: fits the equivalent rule</strong>"],
    ]}
    highlight={[2]}
  />
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    The apple is in the non-black, non-raven cell, so it matches the equivalent wording.
    What remains disputed is whether that makes it evidence for the original claim that
    all ravens are black, and if so how much.
  </Fragment>
</IllustrationFigure>
